-- NamedTuple: the sort of tuple types (proposal 004, its extension of 2026-11-16). A tuple
-- type `(l1: T1, …, ln: Tn)` is this sort applied to its components, one per label, in order;
-- it is written only as tuple syntax.
--
-- Its `PartialEq` and `Eq` are derived: a tuple provides each where every component's type
-- does, and its dictionary holds one sub-dictionary per component, in component order. `eq`
-- compares the components through them. No sort provides `NamedTuple`: the row below is the
-- one the derived instance enters, and nothing provides `Eq` here, since the law at a tuple is
-- its components'. A `PartialEq` a sort writes at a tuple type answers there in place of the
-- derived one, and the tuple's `Eq` is then the writer's to state.
--
-- Its `TypeValue` is derived the same way, where every component's type has one. Its
-- dictionary holds one sub-dictionary per component, in the type's own order, and the
-- labels beside them (`labels: (a, b)`), which `type_value` reads the type back from.
--
-- Its `SortDomain` is derived where every component's type has one: its domain is the tuples
-- of the components' domains, in the type's order. Its `Fillable` dictionary holds each
-- component's `SortDomain` and the labels, which the fill builds the tuple with; its
-- `SortDomain` dictionary holds that `Fillable` in its chain's slot.
sort anthill.prelude.NamedTuple
  operation namedTupleEq(a: NamedTuple, b: NamedTuple) -> Bool @[host_implemented]
  provides anthill.prelude.PartialEq[T = NamedTuple, eq = namedTupleEq]
end
